this is a really common misunderstanding  if you look at how much uv is coming in  and hitting your skin you're actually  getting a bit more than one and a half  times as much uv with spf 30 compared to  spf 50. and these percentages are just  theoretical it's what you get if you  apply exactly two milligrams per square  centimeter completely perfectly evenly  which no one does most people apply a  quarter to a half of what you're meant  to apply which means you're actually  getting four to eight percent uv with  spf 50 and seven to 14 uv with spf 30  and that's on average there'll be spots  where you have a lot less sunscreen but  if you apply the same amount of spf 30  and spf 50 you're always getting a bit  more than one and a half times uv with  spf 30. studies that actually looked at  people playing sunscreen have found that  people burn significantly more with spf  50 compared to spf 100. so the moral of  the story is go with the highest sps  sunscreen that you can apply a lot of  regularly something with the texture  that you like that fits your budget  because if you don't actually wear the  sunscreen it won't work
 

